entries.en:
Don't know where to begin? Have a look at “First program” and “Tiles and coordinates”: they show how to drive the robot and which tile is which. Then try it yourself: stand behind the register, take the order, take a tomato from the table and put it in the bag.

If it just won't work, below are two ready-made scripts: selling tomatoes, and a delivery. They're hidden so as not to spoil the fun: open them only if you really are stuck. Read the `//` comments: they say what each line does. All they need from “Upgrades” is the free `while` card; variables are there from the start.

**The layout.** The scripts are written for this 4 × 4 store (seen from above, the entrance at the bottom): the register's screen at (1, 1), its bag at (2, 1); the tomato table at (1, 3), right above the robot; the garage door in the back wall on the right, over tiles (2, 3) and (3, 3); the robot on (1, 2), behind the register's screen, facing it. In a new store, move the register one tile to the left, the table to (1, 3), and put the robot behind the register's screen. Is yours laid out differently? Then change the directions and steps in the code to fit your store.

@map .Tgg/.Rp./.SB./.c..

>>>! The selling script | The robot takes the order, carries tomatoes from the table to the bag and notes what it sold
The robot serves customers without stopping: it waits for an order, puts as many tomatoes in the bag as are wanted, and crosses off whatever you don't have. It notes every tomato sold in its notebook: you'll order a delivery from those notes.

```
// Work without stopping, customer after customer.
while (true) {
    // Ask the customer at the register for the order (null if none).
    Order order = robot.takeOrder();
    // The robot's notebook: what was sold goes here.
    List<Product> list = robot.getList();
    while (order == null) {          // wait for a customer
        order = robot.takeOrder();
    }
    // While the bag is short of tomatoes:
    while (order.getRemaining(Product.TOMATO) != 0) {
        robot.turn(Direction.UP);        // to the table
        robot.pick(Product.TOMATO);      // take a tomato
        robot.move(Direction.RIGHT);     // to the packing tile
        robot.turn(Direction.DOWN);      // facing the bag
        robot.putInBag(Product.TOMATO);  // into the bag
        robot.move(Direction.LEFT);      // behind the screen
        robot.turn(Direction.DOWN);      // facing the register
        list.add(Product.TOMATO);        // note it down
    }
    // Cross off what we don't have: the customer pays for the bag.
    order.removeRemaining();
}
```

Once the tomatoes on the table run out, the robot will reach for the empty table again and again, with “false: it's empty here” over it. That's your cue: press “Stop”, run the delivery script, then the selling one again. The customer at the register will wait: once the order is taken, they wait as long as it takes.
<<<

>>>! The delivery script | Orders three tomatoes from the notes, takes them from the truck and puts them on the table
Run it once the robot's notebook has at least three tomatoes in it: the selling script notes them there (the robot's panel shows the notebook, the List button). The robot stands behind the register on (1, 2).

```
// The robot's notebook and its first three notes.
List<Product> list = robot.getList();
List<Product> part = list.subList(0, 3);
// Call the delivery. If it fails (false), the note over the robot says why:
// the truck is still busy, or there isn't enough money. Then try again.
while (store.callDelivery(part) == false) {
    robot.turn(Direction.RIGHT);
}
part.clear();                    // ordered: cross these three notes off

robot.move(Direction.RIGHT);     // to the packing tile (2, 2)
robot.move(Direction.UP);        // to the garage door (2, 3)
// Takes the tomatoes once the truck is here and open.
while (store.getRemaining(Product.TOMATO) != 0) {
    robot.pick(Product.TOMATO);
}

robot.move(Direction.DOWN);      // back to the packing tile
robot.move(Direction.LEFT);      // behind the screen (1, 2)
robot.turn(Direction.UP);        // facing the table
robot.putOnShelf(Product.TOMATO);  // three tomatoes on the table
robot.putOnShelf(Product.TOMATO);
robot.putOnShelf(Product.TOMATO);
robot.turn(Direction.DOWN);      // facing the register again
```
